<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires and
    which `bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime` already defaults to `true`. That
    default is why this file has to exist *before* the signing credentials
    do: the hardened runtime denies the camera to an app that has not asked
    for it, so the first notarized build would lose QR sign-in — and lose it
    with the same "camera is unavailable in this browser" wording that
    `NSCameraUsageDescription` was added to fix, on a signed artifact that is
    far more awkward to debug than a local one.

    NSCameraUsageDescription (see Info.plist) and this entitlement are not
    alternatives. The usage description is what macOS shows the user when it
    asks; the entitlement is what permits the app to ask at all. Without the
    description there is no prompt; without the entitlement, under hardened
    runtime, there is no camera.

    Deliberately one entitlement, not a set:

    - No `com.apple.security.device.audio-input`. Nothing here asks for a
      microphone — `browser-qr.ts` requests `audio: false`, and the Linux
      permission handler refuses any request naming audio. Declaring it would
      be claiming a capability the app does not use.
    - No App Sandbox entitlements (`com.apple.security.network.client`,
      `files.user-selected.*`). Those govern the sandbox, which Developer ID
      distribution does not use; the hardened runtime is a different
      mechanism. They would be inert here and misleading to a reader.
      A Mac App Store build (M-W13) would need them, and would need this file
      revisited rather than extended blindly.
    - No `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` or the other `cs.*` relaxations.
      WKWebView runs JavaScript in its own system-provided process, which
      carries its own entitlements; the app does not JIT. Each `cs.*` key
      weakens the hardened runtime, so none is added on suspicion.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
